Chapter: BrotliDictIndex
  cites Compress chapter BrotliDict

 The shape is Lz77's, for the same reason and with the same guarantees: words are
 bucketed by a hash of their first four bytes, and the candidates for a position
 are the chain of words sharing that hash, walked and verified against the actual
 bytes. A chain is a HINT. Every candidate is checked byte for byte before it is
 taken, so a hash collision costs a wasted comparison and never a wrong word, and
 that is what makes the chain limit safe: giving up early can only cost ratio.

 THE INDEX IS BUILT ONCE PER STREAM, NOT ONCE PER POSITION. Building it walks all
 13504 words and allocates about a third of a megabyte; doing that inside the
 matcher's loop would be quadratic in the input. The caller builds it and threads
 it, which is why it is a record rather than something looked up on demand.

 We say:

Section: Shape

 Four thousand buckets against 13504 words keeps the average chain near three.
 Collisions cost only a wasted comparison, because the bytes are checked anyway.

  brdix-hash-size : Integer = 4096
  brdix-hash-mask : Integer = 4095

 How many candidates a position looks at before settling for the best it has.
 Common four-byte prefixes put many words in one chain -- English function words
 share them heavily -- and without a bound a position could walk the whole bucket.

  brdix-chain-limit : Integer = 64

 The shortest word worth taking, and it was MEASURED rather than reasoned about.
 The reasoning said six: a dictionary reference costs an insert-and-copy symbol
 plus a distance code plus fifteen or so extra bits, which a four-byte word looks
 unable to repay against four literals. The measurement disagreed. On the interop
 probe's English text, a floor of six left the case byte-identical -- every word
 in it is three to five bytes, so the floor excluded the entire input -- and the
 three floors go 1754 at six, 1749 at five, 1722 at FOUR, with all nine other
 cases unchanged at every setting.

  brdix-min-word : Integer = 4

  BrDictIx = record {
    bx-head : List Integer,
    bx-next : List Integer,
    bx-off : List Integer,
    bx-len : List Integer,
    bx-data : List Integer,
    bx-n : Integer
  }

Section: Building

  brdix-hash : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  brdix-hash (xs) (p) =
    bit-and (bit-xor (bit-xor (bit-xor (bit-shl (list-at xs p) 12) (bit-shl (list-at xs (p + 1)) 8)) (bit-shl (list-at xs (p + 2)) 4)) (list-at xs (p + 3))) brdix-hash-mask

 The four tables are filled IN PLACE and the slot count is what comes back.
 list-set-at writes the slot and returns the same list, so the caller's tables are
 these tables; copying them per word would allocate a third of a megabyte 13504
 times over. Slots are numbered in corpus order, length by length, so a slot's
 length and offset are what the two side tables say and nothing has to be walked
 to recover them.

  brdix-fill : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  brdix-fill (data) (head) (next) (offs) (lens) (len) (idx) (slot) =
    if len > brdict-max-len then slot
    else if idx >= brdict-count len then brdix-fill data head next offs lens (len + 1) 0 slot
    else let off = brdict-word-at len idx
    in let h = brdix-hash data off
    in let n1 = list-set-at next slot (list-at head h)
    in let h1 = list-set-at head h slot
    in let o1 = list-set-at offs slot off
    in let l1 = list-set-at lens slot len
    in brdix-fill data h1 n1 o1 l1 len (idx + 1) (slot + 1)

 THE TABLES ARE PRE-SIZED, and it is worth more than it looks. A list grown from
 empty doubles as it fills, and every abandoned backing buffer stays on the heap
 until the producing function returns -- there is no collector. Built that way the
 four tables cost 5.5 MB per stream against the 360 KB they actually occupy, and
 that is charged to every compress call of a program that makes many.

  brdix-fill-n : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  brdix-fill-n (n) (v) (acc) =
    if n <= 0 then acc
    else brdix-fill-n (n - 1) v (list-push acc v)

  brdix-sized : Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  brdix-sized (n) (v) = brdix-fill-n n v (__list-with-capacity n)

  brdix-build : List Integer -> BrDictIx
  brdix-build (data) =
    let head = brdix-sized brdix-hash-size (0 - 1)
    in let next = brdix-sized brdict-words (0 - 1)
    in let offs = brdix-sized brdict-words 0
    in let lens = brdix-sized brdict-words 0
    in BrDictIx {
      bx-head = head,
      bx-next = next,
      bx-off = offs,
      bx-len = lens,
      bx-data = data,
      bx-n = brdix-fill data head next offs lens brdict-min-len 0 0
    }

  brdix-none : Integer -> BrDictIx
  brdix-none (ignored) =
    BrDictIx { bx-head = [], bx-next = [], bx-off = [], bx-len = [], bx-data = [], bx-n = 0 }

Section: Lookup

  brdix-eq : List Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  brdix-eq (d) (off) (xs) (p) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then True
    else if list-at d (off + i) == list-at xs (p + i) then brdix-eq d off xs p (i + 1) n
    else False

  brdix-walk : BrDictIx, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  brdix-walk (ix) (xs) (p) (avail) (slot) (fuel) (best) =
    if slot < 0 then best
    else if fuel <= 0 then best
    else let wl = list-at (ix.bx-len) slot
    in let bl = if best < 0 then brdix-min-word - 1 else list-at (ix.bx-len) best
    in let take = if wl > avail then False
       else if wl <= bl then False
       else brdix-eq (ix.bx-data) (list-at (ix.bx-off) slot) xs p 0 wl
    in brdix-walk ix xs p avail (list-at (ix.bx-next) slot) (fuel - 1) (if take then slot else best)

  brdix-find : BrDictIx, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  brdix-find (ix) (xs) (p) (avail) =
    if ix.bx-n <= 0 then 0 - 1
    else if avail < brdix-min-word then 0 - 1
    else brdix-walk ix xs p avail (list-at (ix.bx-head) (brdix-hash xs p)) brdix-chain-limit (0 - 1)

  brdix-slot-len : BrDictIx, Integer -> Integer
  brdix-slot-len (ix) (slot) = list-at (ix.bx-len) slot

  brdix-slot-idx : BrDictIx, Integer -> Integer
  brdix-slot-idx (ix) (slot) =
    let wl = list-at (ix.bx-len) slot
    in (list-at (ix.bx-off) slot - brdict-offset wl) / wl

Section: Transformed Lookup

 A transform can be searched with the SAME chain walk the plain lookup already
 does whenever its output starts with the word's own first bytes -- true exactly
 when it has no prefix and its core is either the word (identity) or a leading
 piece of it (omit-last). That is 54 of the 121, and it is the half that matters
 for prose: a trailing space, " the ", " of ", " and ", ", ", " in ", " to ", a
 full stop, a quote, a newline.

 The other 67 are not searched. The ferment ones would need the input lower-cased
 before hashing; the omit-first ones match a word from the middle, so the hash
 that found the word is the wrong hash and they need an index of their own. Both
 are ratio and not correctness -- the decoder applies all 121, so any stream is
 still read correctly, and a transform we decline to search costs bytes.

 EVERY TABLE HERE ARRIVES AS A RECORD FIELD, NEVER AS A CONSTANT. This loop reads
 the transform tables 121 times per candidate, per chain slot, per input byte, and
 a list constant is rebuilt at every mention: measured, 100000 reads of one
 element of a 121-element constant cost 98.4 MB against 1 KB for the same reads
 through a parameter. The first version of this search read the constants and ran
 the heap into the stack.

  brdix-xf-usable : BrXf, Integer -> Boolean
  brdix-xf-usable (xf) (t) =
    if list-at (xf.bxf-pre-len) t > 0 then False
    else let ty = list-at (xf.bxf-type) t
    in if ty == brdict-xf-identity then True
    else if ty >= brdict-xf-omit-last then True
    else False

  brdix-core-len : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  brdix-core-len (wl) (ty) =
    if ty >= brdict-xf-omit-last then wl - (ty - brdict-xf-omit-last + 1) else wl

  brdix-aff-eq : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  brdix-aff-eq (aff) (off) (n) (xs) (q) (i) =
    if i >= n then True
    else if list-at aff (off + i) == list-at xs (q + i) then brdix-aff-eq aff off n xs q (i + 1)
    else False

 A find answers one integer carrying the slot and the transform:
 `slot * 128 + transform + 1`. The plus one keeps zero free as "nothing found",
 which is unambiguous because slot zero with transform zero is a real answer. How
 many input bytes the pair consumes is NOT packed in -- it is recomputed from the
 slot and the transform at the use site, so there is no field to overflow.

  brdix-pack : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  brdix-pack (slot) (t) = slot * 128 + t + 1

  brdix-slot-of : Integer -> Integer
  brdix-slot-of (packed) = (packed - 1) / 128

  brdix-tf-of : Integer -> Integer
  brdix-tf-of (packed) = (packed - 1) - ((packed - 1) / 128) * 128

 A TRANSFORM IS NOT FREE, AND THE GREEDY RULE COULD NOT SEE THE PRICE. A word
 under transform t has id `index + t * words-at-that-length`, so its distance is
 larger by a factor of t and costs more extra bits to encode. Taking the longest
 match regardless fired on 167 of 191 commands and cost 662 bits MORE than
 transform zero alone -- it was buying one saved literal, worth four or five bits
 under a decent literal code, for eight or ten bits of distance.

  brdix-idbits : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  brdix-idbits (v) (acc) =
    if v <= 0 then acc
    else brdix-idbits (bit-shru v 1) (acc + 1)

  brdix-score : BrDictIx, BrXf, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  brdix-score (ix) (xf) (slot) (t) =
    let wl = list-at (ix.bx-len) slot
    in let used = brdix-core-len wl (list-at (xf.bxf-type) t) + list-at (xf.bxf-suf-len) t
    in used * 8 - brdix-idbits (brdix-slot-idx ix slot + t * brdict-count wl) 0

  brdix-used : BrDictIx, BrXf, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  brdix-used (ix) (xf) (slot) (t) =
    brdix-core-len (list-at (ix.bx-len) slot) (list-at (xf.bxf-type) t) + list-at (xf.bxf-suf-len) t

  brdix-xf-scan : BrDictIx, BrXf, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  brdix-xf-scan (ix) (xf) (xs) (p) (avail) (slot) (t) (best) =
    if t >= brdict-xf-count then best
    else if brdix-xf-usable xf t == False then brdix-xf-scan ix xf xs p avail slot (t + 1) best
    else let cl = brdix-core-len (list-at (ix.bx-len) slot) (list-at (xf.bxf-type) t)
    in let sl = list-at (xf.bxf-suf-len) t
    in let used = cl + sl
    in let bestscore = if best == 0 then 0 - 1000000 else brdix-score ix xf (brdix-slot-of best) (brdix-tf-of best)
    in let ok = if cl < brdix-min-word then False
       else if used > avail then False
       else if brdix-score ix xf slot t <= bestscore then False
       else if brdix-eq (ix.bx-data) (list-at (ix.bx-off) slot) xs p 0 cl then
              brdix-aff-eq (xf.bxf-aff) (list-at (xf.bxf-suf-off) t) sl xs (p + cl) 0
            else False
    in brdix-xf-scan ix xf xs p avail slot (t + 1) (if ok then brdix-pack slot t else best)

  brdix-xf-walk : BrDictIx, BrXf, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  brdix-xf-walk (ix) (xf) (xs) (p) (avail) (slot) (fuel) (best) =
    if slot < 0 then best
    else if fuel <= 0 then best
    else brdix-xf-walk ix xf xs p avail (list-at (ix.bx-next) slot) (fuel - 1) (brdix-xf-scan ix xf xs p avail slot 0 best)

  brdix-find-0 : BrDictIx, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  brdix-find-0 (ix) (xs) (p) (avail) =
    let s = brdix-find ix xs p avail
    in if s < 0 then 0 else brdix-pack s 0

  brdix-find-xf : BrDictIx, BrXf, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  brdix-find-xf (ix) (xf) (xs) (p) (avail) =
    if ix.bx-n <= 0 then 0
    else if avail < brdix-min-word then 0
    else brdix-xf-walk ix xf xs p avail (list-at (ix.bx-head) (brdix-hash xs p)) brdix-chain-limit 0
